DESCRIPTION:Sample:\n\n[W9_HA2017 Proposal-1.docx] (https://um6p.instructur
 e.com/courses/10710/files/841826?wrap=1) \n\nResearch Proposal (group ass
 ignment)\n\n(10% of final mark)\n\nObjective\n\nThis group project  focus
 es on the initial stages of research\, namely topic selection and the stru
 cturing of a pertinent research enquiry. It serves as a foundational step 
 in understanding the fundamentals of structuring a sound inquiry line and 
 composing a robust research proposal. You will  embark on the first step 
 of preparing a collaborative case study project (group assignment 2). \n\
 nThe assignment is designed to foster your research and critical thinking 
 skills while integrating AI as a valuable tool in the preliminary stages o
 f research. Notwithstanding\, it emphasizes the importance of accuracy and
  proper attribution when utilizing AI.\n\nIt also aims to develop essentia
 l research and collaboration skills\, setting the stage for the subsequent
  case study project.\n\nThe ultimate goal of this assignment is to write a
  650-word research proposal for a case study .\n\nCase Study in FPA: in-de
 pth examination of a specific foreign policy process (decision or implemen
 tation)\, used to understand the actors\, motivations\, and internal and e
 xternal constraints that shape foreign policy outcomes.\n\nAssignment over
 view:\n\nPhase 1:  Preliminary Discussions and Brainstorming (with AI ass
 istance)\n\n*\n\nGroup Formation and preliminary topic discussion: Groups 
 (comprising 5-6 students) and a list of 3 potential topics must be finaliz
 ed before Lab session 1 (week 4). Enlist your group via Canvas before this
  session and engage in preliminary discussions about potential research to
 pics beforehand.\n\n*\n\nTopic Selection and research question definition:
  During Lab session 1\, students will collectively decide on their case st
 udy topic taking into consideration the feedback of the instructor. Topics
  must relate to the themes covered in lectures 5 to 10. To ensure diversit
 y\, only 2-3 groups per theme will be accepted\, and case studies within t
 he same theme must differ.\n\n*\n\nUse of AI Tools: AI tools\, such as cha
 tbots\, can be used for topic suggestions and to help the group generate /
  organise initial ideas. However\, the primary focus should be on using AI
  to assist in outlining and organising preliminary thoughts rather than re
 lying mindlessly on AI-generated content.\n\n*\n\nInteractive AI Use: Grou
 ps should use well-crafted prompts to engage with the AI tool\, namely to 
 generate a preliminary list of potential topics within the chosen theme (L
 ecs 5-10)\, and further prompts to explore the viability of suggested case
  studies. These prompts should demonstrate proactiveness in guiding AI to 
 produce context-specific\, depth-appropriate content. The use of AI should
  not replace the due research dilligence to further probe the topic. \n\n
 *\n\nGuidance on Effective AI Prompting: Review this [post ] (https://guid
 es.library.georgetown.edu/ai/prompts)to effectively prompt AI for optimal 
 results.\n\n*\n\nBias and Accuracy Caution: AI chatbots may contain biases
  and inaccuracies. Therefore\, it is crucial to double-check all AI-genera
 ted material for factual accuracy. Groups are responsible for any inaccura
 cies in their final drafts.\n\n*\n\nDocument AI Interactions: keep track o
 f all interactions with the AI chatbot in a separate file\, which must be 
 submitted along with the initial draft.\n\n*\n\nOnce you have a group\, a 
 theme (e.g.\, Media and Public Opinion in FP)\, and a specific topic (e.g.
 \, 'How Trump's tweets influenced US FP regarding the Iran Nuclear Deal')\
 , you can proceed to write your case study proposal.\n\n \n\nPhase 2: Pro
 posal Writing (AI discouraged)\n\n* Collaborative Drafting: Based on the i
 nitial ideas\, each group should collaboratively write a 650-word draft re
 search proposal. While the use of AI is highly discouraged in writing the 
 proposal\, if employed\, proper attribution (e\,g\, highlighting AI-genera
 ted content\, and listing the AI used in the reference list) is mandatory.
  Include the AI chatbot interactions in a separate document as mentioned e
 arlier. \n\n*\n\nThe proposal must be written collaboratively by the tea
 m members.\n\nFor this assignment\, each group will be assigned a shared 
 Google Doc through Canvas>Collaborations. This document must be used thro
 ughout the preparation and writing of your assignment.\n\nPlease follow th
 ese instructions carefully:\n\n* Work directly in your group's shared Goog
 le Doc. All substantive drafting\, writing\, and revision of the assignme
 nt must take place in this document.\n\n* Each student must work using th
 eir own account so that individual contributions can be identified.\n\n* 
 You may conduct research and keep research notes separately\, but do not 
 draft completed sections elsewhere and then paste them into the shared doc
 ument.\n\n* The purpose of using a shared Google Doc is to allow genuine c
 ollaborative writing and to preserve a version history showing how the as
 signment developed and how individual group members contributed.\n\n* The 
 instructor must retain access to the document throughout the assignment. 
 Do not change the sharing settings\, delete the document\, or create a rep
 lacement document.\n\n* The version history may be reviewed when assessin
 g individual participation\, collaborative work\, and compliance with the 
 assignment's academic integrity and AI policy.\n\n \n\nPhase 3: Research 
 Proposal Submission\n\n*\n\nSubmission Deadline: Submit the proposal by th
 e end of week 6 (Sunday\, October 11\, 2026\, by 23:59) via the course pla
 tform (canvas>assignments>research proposal).\n\n*\n\nThe Google Doc under
  Collaborations records your working process\, but you must still form
 ally submit the assignment through the Canvas > Assignment> Research propo
 sal page by the deadline.\n\nOne member of the group should submit the fi
 nal version of the same collaborative document. Do not create a new docum
 ent for submission.\n\nThink of the two as serving different purposes:\n\n
 Canvas Collaboration Google Doc → working process + individual contribut
 ion record\nCanvas Assignment submission → final version for assessment 
 + feedback\n\nAll group members are responsible for checking that the corr
 ect final version has been submitted by the deadline.\n\n*\n\nResearch Pro
 posal Structure:\n\nThe 650-word draft proposal should include:\n\n* Team 
 members' names.\n\n* Preliminary title reflecting your chosen case study (
 CS) topic.\n\n* A well-defined research question (RQ) that clearly reflect
 s your inquiry line.\n\n* The actual proposal text should:\n\n* Provide co
 ntext / background\n\n* Elaborate on the proposed research inquiry (RQ)\n\
 n* Clarify methodology and research design\n\n* Justification of case stud
 y\n\n* Eucidate the CS significance within FPA\n\n* No need to advance arg
 ument at this stage\n\n* A preliminary list of up to 10 academic sources 
 (using the [APA referencing method] (https://apastyle.apa.org/)). AI-sugg
 ested sources must be double-checked and highlighted.\n\n* Proper attribut
 ion to AI-generated content (highlighting AI-generated parts) is mandatory
  as well as reference to other sources utilized in writing / researching t
 he research proposal.\n\nDocuments to be submitted:\n\n* Research Proposal
  (650 words\, excluding the preliminary references). Name your file: <Grou
 p number>_<abbreviated title> (i.e. “G10_Trump Tweets Iran"”)\n\n* Doc
 umented interaction with the AI chatbot: Include the complete dialogue\, f
 rom the first to the last prompt\, covering all phases of the assignment.\
 n\nGrade and feedback will be provided soon after by the instructor.\n\nPl
 ease note that the Proposal to be graded is the one originally submitted v
 ia canvas and not subsequent ones. No exceptions will be entertained\, reg
 ardless changes of topic or improvements based on feedback provided after 
 submission. So ensure that you are certain about the topic and that you ha
 ve consulted with me on any doubts you may have regarding your chosen case
  study prior to submission.\n\nGroups' dynamics: Please note that all grou
 p members receive the same mark. The group is responsible for its own dyna
 mics to ensure an even contribution by all. However\, if you feel the work
 load distribution was unfair despite all efforts by the team\, please get 
 in touch with one of the TAs. Low-performing team members will be downgrad
 ed accordingly.\n\n Academic dishonesty (i.e. plagiarism\, abusive use of
  AI) will result in severe academic consequences. Ensure that all sources\
 , including those generated with the assistance of AI chatbots\, are prope
 rly referenced\, and AI content is clearly attributed (i.e. highlighted) i
 n all documents submitted.\n\n \n\nAssessment Criteria for Case Study Pro
 posal:\n\n \n\nLevels of Attainment\n\nCriteria\n\nExcellent to Outstandi
 ng \n\n18-20\n\n \n\nThe proposal is outstanding. Ideas are extremely we
 ll-articulated and the proposal is well-structured\, strictly following th
 e format recommended.\n\nThe research question is unambiguous and very per
 tinent\, and the research design and methodology are entirely sensible and
  feasible. The proposal is comprehensive yet concise and effectively conve
 ys the research puzzle\, aims the pertinence of the case study\, and its r
 elevance within broader FPA debates.\n\nThe preliminary list of references
  is sensible and denotes a high screening level and familiarity with the r
 elevant literature. Referencing is meticulous and consistent (APA).\n\nStr
 ong and sustained collaboration\, with all members contributing\, discussi
 ng\, and revising each other’s work. The group works collectively rathe
 r than simply dividing the assignment into separate sections\; the version
  history shows sound evidence of members reviewing\, revising\, and buildi
 ng on one another’s contributions.\n\nAI use fully complies with instruc
 tions and remains limited to permitted initial brainstorming / language ed
 iting\; the work clearly demonstrates independent intellectual ownership. 
 Any AI-generated material is transparently identified as required.\n\n \n
 \nGood to very good\n\n14-17\n\n \n\nThe proposal is good. Ideas are in g
 eneral well-articulated and the proposal is sensibly structured\, followin
 g closely the format recommended.\n\nThe research question is sufficiently
  clear and pertinent\, and the research design and methodology are overall
  sensible and feasible. The abstract effectively summarizes the research p
 uzzle\, aims and relevance of the case study\, though some ideas would ben
 efit from more elaboration/clarification.\n\nA preliminary list of referen
 ces is overall sensible and denotes a good level of screening and familiar
 ity with the relevant literature\, though some gaps remain. Referencing ge
 nerally follows APA\, with only minor inconsistencies.\n\n  Good level o
 f intra-group collaboration with meaningful contributions from most member
 s\, although participation or collective revision may be somewhat uneven. 
 The document shows a credible progression from initial ideas to the final 
 proposal\, with evidence of revision and response to feedback.\n\nAI use c
 omplies with instructions and the work remains predominantly student-gener
 ated\, although its use may be less purposeful or clearly documented than 
 at the highest level.\n\nBorderline to Satisfactory\n\n10-13\n\n \n\nThe 
 proposal is adequate. There are issues with the organization/articulation 
 of ideas and the proposal could have been better structured\, as it deviat
 es a little/misses some parts of the format recommended.\n\nThe research q
 uestion is slightly ambiguous and its relevance is questionable. The resea
 rch design and methodology is slightly misaligned with the research questi
 on\, and/or its analytical logic/or feasibility is somewhat questionable. 
 The abstract could have been more effective in summarizing the research pu
 zzle\, aims and relevance of the case study\, or is incomplete\, or confus
 ing.\n\nThe preliminary list of references is sensible but incomplete and 
 denotes a superficial level of screening.  Sources referencing do not al
 l comply with APA style rules (lack of consistency).\n\n Collaboration is
  limited or uneven\, with substantial task-splitting and little evidence o
 f members engaging critically with each other’s contributions. The versi
 on history shows some drafting and revision\, but the development process 
 is relatively limited or concentrated close to the deadline.\n\nAI use bro
 adly complies with the rules\, but there may be weak documentation\, over
 reliance across all phases of the proposal\, or insufficient distinction b
 etween AI-assisted and independently produced material.\n\nReferences are
  incomplete or superficial\, showing only limited screening of relevant l
 iterature. Referencing has noticeable APA inconsistencies\; some sources m
 ay be unreliable.\n\nFailed\n\n1-9\n\n \n\nThe proposal lacks in a majori
 ty of areas. Ideas are poorly organized and articulated\, and the structur
 e of the proposal deviates substantially from the format recommended.\n\nT
 he research question is ambiguous and lacks pertinence. The research desig
 n and methodology are completely misaligned with the research question\, a
 nd its analytical logic/or feasibility is problematic. The abstract fails 
 to summarize the research puzzle\, aims and relevance of the case study\, 
 and the organization of ideas is very confusing.\n\nLittle evidence of gen
 uine collaborative work or independent intellectual engagement. Significan
 t task-splitting\, no collective revision. The version history provides li
 ttle evidence of collective discussion\, revision\, or progressive develop
 ment of the work.\n\nThere is evidence of AI use beyond the permitted bra
 instorming phase\, undisclosed AI-generated material\, or substantial reli
 ance on AI in producing the content or analysis.\n\nThe preliminary list o
 f references denotes a very superficial screening level and poor familiari
 ty with the relevant literature. The references are minimal\, inappropria
 te (mostly blogs\, websites\, newspapers)\, or poorly integrated\, demons
 trating weak engagement with relevant literature.  Referencing is incons
 istent or absent (e.g.\, raw links\, non-APA style).\n\n \n\nUnacceptabl
 e\n\n0\n\nNo proposal submitted\n\nor\n\nplagiarised proposal
SEQUENCE:0
